Theorem 6.2 states that the closed subsets of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\) are exactly \(\{x \mid f(x) = \bot\}\) for continuous \(f\), with typical set \(\{\bot\}\). The same note asserts the identity \(\{\top\} = \bigcap_{n \in \omega}\{x \mid n \in x\}\), and concludes that \(\{\top\}\) is a countable intersection of open sets. `theorem_6_2` is the biconditional for membership in `ScottF`, the class of complements of Scott-open sets: the top characteristic of the complement is Scott continuous and equals \(\bot\) exactly on a closed set, while if the set is \(\{x \mid f(x) = \bot\}\) then its complement is the union over \(k\) of the Scott-open sets \(\{x \mid k \in f(x)\}\), using that \(f(x)\) differs from \(\bot\) exactly when some \(k\) belongs to it and that no \(k\) belongs to \(\bot\). `typicalF_mem_ScottF` applies this to the identity, and `typicalF_universal` rewrites the biconditional as continuous preimages of \(\{\bot\}\), since membership in that singleton is equality with \(\bot\). Neither gloss proves \(\{\top\} = \bigcap_n \{x \mid n \in x\}\). **Theorem:** partial — the \(\mathfrak{F}\) biconditional is `theorem_6_2`, and the displayed identity for \(\{\top\}\) is not among the glosses. **Proof:** partial — the complement-and-union argument proves the biconditional, and no gloss proves the intersection formula.
